// Decadence (Rite) - the second of two readings of the main theme from Giten
// Megami Tensei (1995). decadence.score is the first: same form as the
// original, new instruments, and the low end sorted out. This one keeps all of
// that and stops treating the original's thirty-two bars as a fixed thing.
//
// Two changes, and both of them are about the piece having an arc rather than
// a level. A collapse section, `hollow`, opens eight bars of near-silence
// before the climax - see the comment above it. And the built chord no longer
// breathes four identical times per section: the fourth time it goes up it
// stays up, holding through into the next half instead of letting go. The
// first three breaths are the gesture; the fourth one refusing to exhale is
// what makes them a phrase instead of a loop.
//
// Everything below this point is shared with decadence.score, including the
// whole account of what the original is and what a MIDI transcription could
// not carry across.
//
// What the original is. D minor with a raised fourth - G# against the D -
// at 120 beats a minute, thirty-two bars and then round again. It is two
// halves that never share a chord:
//
//   A  a choir drones on D for sixteen bars. Over it a "voice" synth builds
//      one chord a note at a time, D, then A, then G#, then B, each held
//      while the next arrives, the whole stack let go every two bars and
//      built again. A bright synth plays the melody, a handful of notes
//      each held nearly two bars: C, a G# pickup, A; D, G#, A; then a
//      phrase that climbs D C D E F G# A C. The G# is the colour of the
//      piece - a raised fourth in D minor, Hungarian rather than aeolian,
//      the sound of somewhere old and wrong.
//   B  the melody stops. The bass walks a semitone down and back, D C# C
//      C#, two bars each; the voice rocks on every beat between two-note
//      chords a semitone apart; and a warm pad builds a chord from the
//      bottom up over eight bars, A C F C - then again on G Bb Eb Bb, an Eb
//      major over the D, the flat second, the darkest chord in the key.
//
//   drums  a kick and a ride together, once every two bars, with a kick
//          and an open hat snatched in just before every other downbeat; in
//          the second half, on every half note. A toll more than a beat.
//
// What changes. The sounds, and the second time round.
//
//   The melody is Halo, a glassy synth with a bell's inharmonic strike in
//   it, doubled by tubular bells: what the old "brightness" patch was, a
//   pad with a struck edge, and a church in it. The drone is a male choir
//   over a sub, the building chord a synthetic voice with a real choir
//   inside it, the rocking chords the synthetic voice alone - a sampled
//   choir cannot speak on every beat. The warm pad is the full string
//   section. The ride is an acoustic ride among electronic drums, because
//   the toll is the one thing in the old kit that has to ring.
//
//   The loop is the original's thirty-two bars played twice. The first
//   time is the original, instrument for instrument. The second time the
//   beat comes in under it - half-time, a clap on three - and when the
//   melody returns, sopranos sing it an octave above the bells.
//
// Form. A short intro of the drone and the tolls plays once; `hollow` empties
// the piece out between the two loud halves; `end` lets the melody's C fall to
// A over the built chord, and rings off.
//
// What a General MIDI transcription cannot carry. The notes came across
// faithfully and the spectrum did not, because in 1995 "bass" was a thin GM
// patch and the kick was a thin GM drum. Neither carried real sub, so writing
// them on the same beats cost nothing. Played on Drift and on Circuit's kick
// they are both full-bandwidth in the bottom octave, and the same rhythm now
// means two sources fighting for it - measured at 60.3 dB against 59.6 below
// 120 Hz, and 65-84% of the energy in every window the limiter pulled down.
// That is the distortion, and it is not a bug anywhere in the engine: it is
// an arrangement that was never asked who owns the low end at a given moment.
// The answer here is the drone. See `toll` for where the kick went instead.
//
// The other half of the same problem is that every lane attacked on the same
// grid. Correlated attacks sum linearly - at the worst instant, 100% of the
// amplitude was lanes pushing the same direction, nothing opposing - so the
// mix peaked far above what any of it sounded like. The pockets spread the
// mid and high attacks across about twenty-eight milliseconds: the lead ahead
// at -8, the choir's built chord at +6, the bass at +12, the strings latest
// at +20. It is not swing and it is not meant to be heard as timing; it is
// the difference between a chord and a click. `pulse` is deliberately
// left out of it, at feel:tight - it is a machine, and it should sound like
// one against everything around it that breathes.
//
// `keys.root` also moved up an octave, d3 to d4. The bass role's male choir
// is transposed +12 and was already singing d3; the built chord was doubling
// it rather than sitting over it.

title: Decadence (Rite)
ensemble: decadence
parts: lead high:choir@lead keys pulse:keys@keys bass pad perc metal:perc@perc
tempo: 120
meter: 4/4
key: d minor
order: intro [a b a2 hollow b2] end

// Bars the sections share. A pattern plays as if written out where it is used.
pattern toll        // the kick: a bar on, a bar off, a push into the next
  | kick:4! | r:4 | kick:4! | r:3.75 kick:0.25. |
pattern lift        // the open hat that lifts into every fourth bar
  | r:4 | r:4 | r:4 | r:3.75 hat_open:0.25 |
// What the second time round adds. Every one of them is the toll made heavier
// and never the toll made busier: nothing in this piece subdivides. The ride
// goes from every other bar to every bar and then to half notes, and a clap
// arrives on three. The kick does not change at all, and that is deliberate.
//
// It used to. A `knell` put it on every downbeat under both repeats, and that
// is what was driving the limiter. Below 120 Hz the kick and the sub are the
// same size - 60.3 dB against 59.6 across b2 - and 65-84% of the energy in
// every window the limiter pulled down was under 120 Hz. `toll` puts the kick
// on the bar the bass turns over and leaves the bar between it alone, so the
// transient lands while the sub's envelope is still climbing rather than on
// top of it at full sustain. The weight the repeats gain is all above the
// kick: ride, clap, crash, and one boom a section.
pattern tolling     // one ride a bar, the bell on the first of every four
  | ride_bell:4! | ride:4 | ride:4 | ride:4 |
pattern tolling2    // half notes, the bell still only every fourth bar
  | ride_bell:2! ride:2 | ride:2 ride:2 | % | % |
pattern three       // a clap on three and nothing else: the half-time backbeat
  | r:2 clap:2! | % | % | % |
pattern melody
  | c5:7_ g#4:1 | a4:8_ |
  | d4:7_ g#4:1 | a4:8_ |
  | c5:7_ g#4:1 | a4:4_ d4:3_ c4:1 |
  | d4:3_ e4:1 f4:3_ g#4:1 | a4:4_ c5:4_ |
// The same melody, sung rather than struck. Every note the bells hold for a
// bar or more is bowed instead of laid flat; the passing notes of the climb
// keep their tenuto, because a voice does not swell on a quarter note. Not a
// note and not an onset differs from `melody` - only what happens to a note
// after it starts. The sopranos take this and the bells keep the original,
// so the second statement is the same tune stated twice at once, once struck
// and once breathed.
pattern melody2
  | c5:7^ g#4:1 | a4:8^ |
  | d4:7^ g#4:1 | a4:8^ |
  | c5:7^ g#4:1 | a4:4^ d4:3_ c4:1 |
  | d4:3_ e4:1 f4:3_ g#4:1 | a4:4^ c5:4^ |


// The drone swells in and the ride tolls; in the last two bars the root of
// the voice's chord joins, so the A section's first build arrives mid-breath.
section intro bars:4
  bass dyn:pp..mf pocket:12
    | d2:16^ |
  keys.root dyn:pp..p feel:loose pocket:6
    | r:4 | r:4 | d3:8 |
  perc.feet dyn:p..mp feel:tight
    | toll |
  metal dyn:p..mp feel:tight
    | ride:4 | r:4 | ride:4 | r:4 |
  perc.right dyn:p..mp feel:tight
    | lift |
  perc.drop dyn:mp
    | boom:4! | r:4 | r:4 | r:4 |


// The original's first half, instrument for instrument: drone, built chord,
// the melody on bells, the toll.
section a bars:16
  bass dyn:mp pocket:12
    | d2:64 |
  // The original lets the whole stack go every two bars and builds it again.
  // On the synth it was written for that is a gesture; on a sampled choir it
  // is a hole. All four voices used to release on the same beat, and with a
  // 1.25s release and a 0.32s attack the chord faded out, left about a
  // quarter of a second of nothing, and swelled back - four times a section.
  // That is the unnatural pause.
  //
  // So the choir stagger-breathes, which is what a choir does. The root never
  // rests at all; it re-takes the D every four bars under the others and
  // there is no gap to hear. The three above it leave one at a time, an
  // eighth apart, and come back one at a time, so the stack still empties to
  // the ground and rebuilds from it - the gesture survives, the hole does
  // not. The sixth's notes are also longer than they were: at a beat and a
  // half the old b3 was released before it had finished arriving, and what
  // came out was not a note but a bump.
  // The gap in the build is the original's: channel 1 releases all four
  // voices on beat 8 and does not rebuild until beat 11, three beats later,
  // then stacks the rebuild one beat per voice. On a 1995 synth voice that
  // reads as a breath. On the sampled choir it does not, because the attack
  // here is 0.32s at mp - a voice re-entering a beat after the one below it
  // is still climbing when the next arrives, so the rebuild never speaks and
  // the hole stays a hole.
  //
  // So the release is slurred across it instead. Each voice holds from its
  // own entry straight through to where it used to re-enter - the fifth to
  // beat 11, the third to 12, the sixth to 13 - and `~` carries it into the
  // second note rather than letting the tail decay first. The re-articulation
  // is still there and still one beat apart; what is gone is the silence in
  // front of it. The chord now thins from the top down across beats 8-13 and
  // refills from the bottom up, which is the same gesture without the pause.
  // The build, as 1995 wrote it. Channel 1 puts all four voices on - two beats
  // apart, bottom up - then lets all four go together on beat 8, and rebuilds
  // from the bottom a beat at a time. The letting-go is the motif. An earlier
  // pass here slurred across it to close the gap, which removed the gesture
  // instead of fixing how it sounded; with the halo lead pulled back it is
  // audible that nothing lets go any more.
  //
  // So the release is back and the gap is not. The original rests three beats
  // before rebuilding, which a 1995 synth voice can do because it speaks
  // instantly; this choir's attack is 0.32s at mp, so three beats of silence
  // followed by entries one beat apart is a hole followed by a smear. The
  // rebuild starts at 9.5 instead of 11 - a beat and a half of air rather than
  // three - and keeps the original's one-beat spacing. Same gesture, half the
  // hole, and every voice gets to finish arriving before the next one lands.
  //
  // The root is d3 again, the original's octave, and it re-articulates with
  // the rest instead of holding through the cell. It had been at d4 since the
  // low-end work, to keep it off the male choir the bass role transposes up to
  // d3 - that clash is real and is measured in the commit. What it cost was
  // the bottom of the chord sitting out the figure entirely.
  keys.root dyn:mp feel:loose pocket:6
    | d3:8 r:1.5 d3:6.5 | % | % | d3:16 |
  keys.fifth dyn:mp feel:loose pocket:6
    | r:2 a3:6 r:2.5 a3:5.5 | % | % | r:2 a3:14 |
  keys.raised dyn:mp feel:loose pocket:6
    | r:4 g#3:4 r:3.5 g#3:4.5 | % | % | r:4 g#3:12 |
  keys.sixth dyn:mp feel:loose pocket:6
    | r:6 b3:2 r:4.5 b3:3.5 | % | % | r:6 b3:10 |
  lead dyn:mp..mf pocket:-8
    | melody |
  perc.feet dyn:mp feel:tight
    | toll*4 |
  metal dyn:mp feel:tight
    | ride:4 r:4 | % | % | % | % | % | % | % |
  perc.right dyn:mp feel:tight
    | lift*4 |


// The second half: the bass walks, the voice rocks, the strings build A C F C
// and then the Eb chord over D.
section b bars:16
  bass dyn:mf pocket:12
    | d2:8 | c#2:8 | c2:8 | c#2:8 |
    | d2:8 | c#2:8 | c2:8 | c#2:8 |
  // The 1995 file settles this. The chorus there is one instrument, GM
  // program 85, and it plays BOTH halves of the tune: it builds the chord for
  // sixteen bars and then rocks this pulse for sixteen more, without ever
  // handing off. Here it was handing off, because this lane (then `keys.pulse`)
  // named a voice, which played only its own layers - it reached `vox_pulse`, a synth,
  // and never the sampled choir sitting in the same role. At the forty-second
  // mark the choir stopped being a choir. That is a casting decision that
  // lived in decadence.json where the score cannot show it.
  //
  // The ensemble now sends the choir here too, and the synth drops from +9 dB to
  // +2 so it colours the attack instead of being the sound. The motif itself
  // does not need to be pasted into this section: it is already here, in the
  // strings below - a3/c4/f4/c5 entering two bars apart and releasing
  // together is exactly the original's pad, and exactly the gesture `a` opens
  // with. An earlier pass doubled that build on the choir as well; with the
  // choir actually reaching the pulse the double was two of the same thing.
  pulse dyn:p..mp feel:tight
    | [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. |
    | [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. |
    | [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. |
    | [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. |
    | [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. |
    | [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. |
    | [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. |
    | [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. |
  // The build, bowed. Each voice enters two bars after the one below it and
  // holds to the end of its half, so the four notes are already a staircase;
  // what they were not was played. `^` takes a note under its written level,
  // up past it and back before it releases, and the strings can only do that
  // now that they are sent expression rather than the mod wheel, which the
  // Virtual Playing Orchestra patches declare nothing for - see the layer
  // controls in decadence.json.
  //
  // The swell peaks at four fifths of a note's length, so the later a voice
  // enters the later it crests: the floor at 12.8s into its eight bars, the
  // third at 13.6, the fifth at 14.4, the top at 15.2. The chord does not
  // arrive all at once, it arrives from the bottom up over the last three
  // seconds, which is what the entries were always spelling and what a flat
  // hold could not say. The floor is the exception in the first half - it is
  // the ground the others are measured against, so it is held, not bowed.
  //
  // Second time the chord is Eb over D, the flat second, the darkest thing in
  // the key. Everything bows there, the floor included, and the Eb itself is
  // the one note taken with an accent. Not louder - the written level does
  // not change - but arrived at rather than appearing.
  pad.floor dyn:mp..mf feel:loose pocket:20
    | a3:32_ | g3:32^ |
  pad.third dyn:mp..mf feel:loose pocket:20
    | r:8 | c4:24^ | r:8 | bb3:24^ |
  pad.fifth dyn:mp..mf feel:loose pocket:20
    | r:16 | f4:16^ | r:16 | eb4:16!^ |
  pad.top dyn:mp..mf feel:loose pocket:20
    | r:24 | c5:8^ | r:24 | bb4:8^ |
  perc.feet dyn:mp feel:tight
    | toll*4 |
  metal dyn:mf feel:tight
    | ride:2*2 | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % |
  perc.right dyn:mf feel:tight
    | r:4 r:3.75 hat_open:0.25 | % | % | % | % | % | % | % |


// The first half again, with the beat under it, and sopranos an octave over
// the bells.
section a2 bars:16
  bass dyn:mf pocket:12
    | d2:64 |
  // The same four entries, breathing. In `a` the built chord appears: each
  // note arrives at its written level and stays there until the stack is let
  // go. Here each note is bowed in, so the chord is never at rest - it is
  // always either arriving or leaving, and it rebuilds eight times across the
  // section instead of standing still four times. The sopranos over the bells
  // swell too, but the bells sit on top of them and the ear mostly hears the
  // strike; this is the lane where the second statement can actually be heard
  // to be a second statement. Again: same notes, same entries, same written
  // level as `a`. Only what happens after each note starts.
  keys.root dyn:mf feel:loose pocket:6
    | d3:8^ r:1.5 d3:6.5^ | % | % | d3:16^ |
  keys.fifth dyn:mf feel:loose pocket:6
    | r:2 a3:6^ r:2.5 a3:5.5^ | % | % | r:2 a3:14^ |
  keys.raised dyn:mf feel:loose pocket:6
    | r:4 g#3:4^ r:3.5 g#3:4.5^ | % | % | r:4 g#3:12^ |
  keys.sixth dyn:mf feel:loose pocket:6
    | r:6 b3:2^ r:4.5 b3:3.5^ | % | % | r:6 b3:10^ |
  lead.main dyn:mf pocket:-8
    | melody |
  high dyn:mp..mf pocket:-8
    | melody2 |
  perc.fx dyn:mf
    | crash:4! |
    | r:4 | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % |
  perc.drop dyn:mf
    | boom:4! |
    | r:4 | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % |
  perc.feet dyn:mp feel:tight
    | toll*4 |
  perc.left dyn:mf feel:tight
    | three*4 |
  perc.right dyn:mf feel:tight
    | lift*4 |
  metal dyn:mf feel:tight
    | tolling*4 |


// The floor drops out.
//
// This is the section the original does not have, and the reason this file
// exists next to decadence.score. That piece escalates from the intro to the
// last bar without ever getting quiet: thirty-two bars of everything, and an
// ear with nothing to measure them against stops hearing them as loud and
// starts hearing them as constant. The congestion at the climax was never only
// a level problem. Half of it was that nothing had been soft for a minute.
//
// So a2 ends on a boom and then there is almost nothing for eight bars. The
// bass keeps walking - it is the one thing that carries over, so the collapse
// reads as the piece continuing rather than the piece stopping - and the bells
// state the melody's first phrase once, alone, with no chord under it and no
// beat around it. The built chord is gone entirely: a held D over the walk
// would put a minor ninth against the C#, which in `b` the rocking voice
// covers by moving, and here there is nothing to cover it with. That absence
// is the point.
//
// Two cymbals mark it out: one where the floor goes, one in the last bar as
// the only warning. Then b2 arrives into silence rather than into more.
section hollow bars:8
  bass dyn:mf..pp pocket:12
    | d2:8 | c#2:8 | c2:8 | c#2:8 |
  lead dyn:mp..pp pocket:-8
    | c5:7_ g#4:1 | a4:8_ | r:8 | r:8 |
  metal dyn:mp..p feel:tight
    | ride:4! r:4 | r:8 | r:8 | r:4 ride:4! |
  perc.drop dyn:mf
    | boom:4! r:4 | r:8 | r:8 | r:8 |


// The second half with everything, arriving after eight bars of nothing.
section b2 bars:16
  bass dyn:mf pocket:12
    | d2:8 | c#2:8 | c2:8 | c#2:8 |
    | d2:8 | c#2:8 | c2:8 | c#2:8 |
  pulse dyn:mp feel:tight
    | [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. |
    | [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. |
    | [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. |
    | [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. |
    | [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. |
    | [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. |
    | [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. [a3 c4]:1 [g#3 b3]:1. |
    | [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. [g3 bb3]:1 [f#3 a3]:1. |
  // The build again, and this is where the two halves of the piece part. In
  // `b` the floor is held while the three voices above it bow - it is the
  // ground the staircase is measured against. Here nothing is ground: the
  // floor bows too, in both halves, so the whole chord is moving the entire
  // time and there is no fixed thing to stand on. Every voice above it takes
  // its entry with an accent, so the staircase is climbed rather than
  // revealed.
  //
  // The written levels sit at mp..mf, under `b`'s, and that is deliberate.
  // Four voices bowing together crest together, and at mf..f they crested
  // seven decibels above the rest of the piece and took the limiter down
  // with them - which pulled the drums and the melody down too, so the
  // loudest passage arrived as the most congested one. What makes this the
  // second time round is the bowing and the accents, not the level. Strip
  // the dynamics out and `b2` would still be the more frightening of the
  // two, because the chord under it will not hold still. It does not need
  // to be louder as well, and the mix cannot afford it.
  pad.floor dyn:mp..mf feel:loose pocket:20
    | a3:32^ | g3:32^ |
  pad.third dyn:mp..mf feel:loose pocket:20
    | r:8 | c4:24!^ | r:8 | bb3:24!^ |
  pad.fifth dyn:mp..mf feel:loose pocket:20
    | r:16 | f4:16!^ | r:16 | eb4:16!^ |
  pad.top dyn:mp..mf feel:loose pocket:20
    | r:24 | c5:8!^ | r:24 | bb4:8!^ |
  perc.fx dyn:mf
    | crash:4! |
    | r:4 | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % |
  perc.drop dyn:mf
    | boom:4! |
    | r:4 | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % | % |
  perc.feet dyn:mf feel:tight
    | toll*4 |
  perc.left dyn:mf feel:tight
    | three*4 |
  perc.right dyn:mf feel:tight
    | r:4 r:3.75 hat_open:0.25 | % | % | % | % | % | % | % |
  metal dyn:mf feel:tight
    | tolling2*4 |


// The way out: back to D, the chord built once more under the melody's C,
// which falls to A and rings off with the ride.
section end bars:4
  bass dyn:mf..p pocket:12
    | d2:16 |
  keys.root dyn:mf..p feel:loose pocket:6
    | d3:16 |
  keys.fifth dyn:mf..p feel:loose pocket:6
    | r:1 a3:15 |
  keys.raised dyn:mf..p feel:loose pocket:6
    | r:2 g#3:14 |
  keys.sixth dyn:mf..p feel:loose pocket:6
    | r:3 b3:13 |
  lead dyn:mf..p pocket:-8
